        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "refactor(macos): move PortGuardian state to SQLite",
        "body": "Stacked on #110392. Review and merge after that PR.\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nResolves a problem where the macOS PortGuardian orphan-tunnel ledger remains in a host-global JSON file even though OpenClaw already ships a canonical SQLite table for the same durable records. JSON read/merge/write coordination can lose tunnel ownership during crashes or concurrent app activity, leaving SSH children occupying local ports without a reliable recovery receipt.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nPortGuardian now uses the existing `macos_port_guardian_records` table in the host-global OpenClaw state database. The logical record shape remains unchanged: `pid`, `port`, `command`, `mode`, and `timestamp`. This is additive at the schema and API boundaries: no table change, schema-version bump, wire-format change, or dual-write path.\n\nThe shared Apple SQLite bootstrap/validation code moves into `OpenClawNativeState`, so device identity and PortGuardian compose the same exact canonical version-zero tables before Node expands the database to the full schema.\n\nLegacy `port-guard.json` is a one-time migration input only. Migration snapshots under the shipped Darwin lock, plans process reconciliation outside both the file lock and SQLite transaction, revalidates the exact source, applies a short synchronous transaction, verifies receipts, and retires the JSON file. Runtime then reads and writes SQLite only.\n\nThis migration deliberately lives in native runtime, unlike ordinary Doctor-owned state migration. Older shipped macOS writers coordinate through the Darwin file lock and can appear while the app is running. Only the native owner can participate in that lock, apply the Security.framework/CDHash mixed-copy gate, finish asynchronous process inspection before the transaction, then reacquire and revalidate the authoritative source for a short synchronous SQLite commit. Node Doctor cannot safely provide those native coordination or code-signing guarantees.\n\nMixed-version app copies fail closed. A signed storage-capability marker is accepted only when Security.framework metadata matches the running process kernel CDHash. Tunnel launch holds a guardian reservation from completed legacy reconciliation through process spawn and durable receipt commit; no concurrent store open or migration can enter that unrecorded window.\n\n## User Impact\n\nmacOS SSH tunnel ownership and orphan cleanup become transactionally durable across concurrent app instances, PID reuse, crashes, delayed teardown, and legacy migration. Fresh installs no longer create the JSON ledger or lock sidecar.\n\nThis is a one-way storage cutover. Downgrading afterward to an older JSON-only app does not expose receipts written only to SQLite; rollback requires restoring a compatible backup or upgrading again.\n\n## Maintainer Decision\n\nApprove both deliberate contracts before merge:\n\n1. The native-runtime migration exception above, bounded to interoperability with the shipped Darwin lock and signed mixed-copy detection.\n2. The one-way downgrade/mixed-version contract: old JSON-only copies must not share the migrated state directory.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Exact rebased head: `0fa751207e10debaba47e22e6840e09d9cf499db`; clean stacked merge onto #110392.\n- Developer-ID-signed candidate embedded that exact head and storage marker `2`; released v2026.7.1 was independently signature-valid and had no marker.\n- Disposable macOS VM, real signed apps: mixed-version exclusion preserved the legacy ledger and live SSH child without opening the SQLite table; one-time migration retired JSON and preserved one exact receipt; a second launch left it unchanged; SQLite orphan recovery killed the orphan and compare-deleted its receipt.\n- Signed-app terminal proof ended `SIGNED_PORTGUARDIAN_PROOF=PASS`. Unified logs showed the legacy-copy gate and `reaped orphaned ssh tunnel (pid 3680, local port 43123)`.\n- 47 focused Swift tests passed across `PortGuardianRecordStoreTests`, `LowCoverageHelperTests`, and `RemotePortTunnelTests`, including concurrency, rollback, schema validation, migration races, spawn reservation, PID reuse, and a real orphan process.\n- Remote exact-head proof: 87 Node state-database tests and 489 database-first guard tests passed; full changed-surface type, lint, format, boundary, docs, and package gates passed.\n- Fresh branch autoreview reported no accepted/actionable findings; patch correct confidence 0.84.\n- VM proof was guest-only. The original snapshot was restored and the VM stopped; no host runtime state was touched.\n\nAI assistance was used for implementation and review. Maintainer-directed design, focused tests, exact-head remote validation, and signed native behavior proof were performed before handoff.\n",
